    val sections: List<ManualSection> = listOf(
        ManualSection("A first song", "Tracks, scenes, clips, and a first sound.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A song has up to sixteen tracks. Each track has one machine on it: a synth, a drum machine, a sampler and so on. The track and its instrument are the same thing, so there's no separate instrument list."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The song grid"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The first screen is the song grid. Tracks run down the left, one per row, and scenes run across the top, one per column. Where a track and a scene meet is a clip: the notes that track plays in that scene."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The bar along the bottom is on every screen. Its right end always has the same five buttons in the same order: undo, redo, mixer, record and play."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Making a sound"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Step, "Tap **+ track** and pick a machine. A new song already has one."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Step, "Tap the empty clip where the track meets the first scene. This opens the editor."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Step, "Play the keyboard at the bottom. Tap **patch** at the top of the panel to try the factory sounds."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Step, "Draw notes in the grid, or press record and play them in."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Step, "Tap the back arrow to go back to the song, and press play."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Scenes"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A scene is a section of the song, like an intro or a verse. All the clips in a scene play together, and the scene repeats as many times as its header says before the song moves on."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Clips in a scene don't have to be the same length. A one-bar drum clip under a four-bar chord clip just loops four times."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Saving"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The song you're working on is saved as you go and comes back next time you open the app. **save** in the header saves it under its name. **file** has new song, save as, and your other songs."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The demo songs"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The first time the app opens it loads a demo song to look around in and pull apart. **file · Demo songs** has five, each in a different style:"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Riddim** - dub. Scenes, swing, a sidechain, a group, and a siren drawn as a bend."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Squelch** - acid house. The acid bass with accents and slides, a filter sweep, and the perform effects recorded into the song."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Night Drive** - synthwave. FM bass, gated reverb, a lead with vibrato, and scenes that fade in and out."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Cartridge** - chiptune. The chip machine on every track, and a scene in 7/8."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Lantern** - chamber music in 3/4. The modelled strings, woodwinds and horn, slowing down at the end."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Picking one loads a fresh copy. It replaces the open song like opening any other song does, and isn't saved until you save it, so a song of yours with the same name is safe."),
        )),
        ManualSection("The song grid", "The arranger, the launcher, and freezing.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The grid works in two ways. The square in its top left corner switches between them."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "As an arranger"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "This is the default. The song plays scene by scene from left to right. Each scene plays all its clips, repeats as many times as its header says, and then the next scene starts."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap a scene's header to play just that scene."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Hold a scene's header for its menu: settings, insert, duplicate, delete, and move left or right."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The loop button at the left of the bottom bar: **tap** it to choose what loops, the whole song or the current scene. **Hold** it to choose whether it loops at all: **⟳** loops forever, **⇥ end** plays through once and stops."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Stop means stop. The next play starts from the top of the song."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "### A scene's tempo"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "In a scene's settings, **tempo** can follow the song or be the scene's own. An own tempo either jumps in when the scene starts or glides in over its first bar."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Ramp at end** changes the tempo inside the scene: to the bpm set by **to**, over the last bars set by **over**. Slow down into the next scene, or speed up across a whole one. It happens on the scene's last time through, so a scene that repeats four times only slows at the very end. The next scene then starts at its own tempo. The header shows ↘ or ↗ on a scene with a ramp, and a MIDI export writes it as a tempo change on every beat."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "As a launcher"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Tap the corner square and the grid becomes a clip launcher. Each track plays its own clip, from any scene."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap a clip to launch it. It starts on the next beat line rather than straight away, so it lands in time."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The **q:** button sets what it waits for. **end** waits for the playing clip to finish its loop. The others wait for a number of bars."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap a scene's header to launch that whole column."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap a playing clip to stop it at the end of its loop. Tap stop twice to stop everything."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Each track keeps its own position, and stop leaves them where they are. The readout above the bottom bar shows where each one is."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "### Looping"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "In the launcher, an empty cell is a looper."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap an empty cell. It becomes a clip, starts on the next line, and records what you play into it. The cell's edge pulses red while it records."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap it again to close the loop. It ends on the nearest bar line. If nobody taps, it closes itself at 16 bars."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Once closed it goes on recording on top of itself each time round, with a steady red edge. Tap to stop adding to it, and tap again to add more."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "To have loops close themselves at a set length, pick one under **loops record for** in the **q:** window."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "What you end up with is an ordinary clip. Double tap it to edit it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Zooming"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Drag with two fingers to move around the grid, and pinch to make the cells bigger or smaller. One finger still opens and launches clips."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Clip settings"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Hold a clip for its settings: its length in bars, mute, and the grid it snaps to."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Copy, cut, paste and clear** are at the top. You can hold any cell to get them, including an empty one, so you can paste a copied clip into another scene or another track. Paste replaces the whole clip, so pasting over a clip asks first, and so does clear. Cut doesn't ask, because the clip is still on the clipboard."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A copy includes the notes, automation and settings, including its length. It doesn't include frozen audio, so a pasted clip plays its machine until you freeze it again. Recordings on an audio track do come along."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "What a cell shows"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The number of bars is in the corner, with **1** for a one-shot and **M** for a muted clip. A cell on an audio track shows its waveform and how many of its four lanes have a recording. It turns **amber** if one was recorded at a different tempo from the scene's, because audio doesn't stretch there."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Freezing"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Freezing renders a clip to audio and plays that instead of the machine, which frees up the CPU for everything else. You can freeze one clip, a whole scene or a whole track from the menus."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The track's two insert effects are frozen in as well, so a frozen clip costs almost nothing to play. The mixer stays live: fader, pan, sends and mute all still work."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The tail (reverb, delay, long release) is rendered too and kept separately, so it rings over the next loop and keeps ringing when the clip stops, the same as the live machine would. The render keeps going until the sound has died away, up to eight seconds."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "If a scene ramps smoothly to a new tempo, frozen clips in it are time-stretched through the ramp without changing pitch."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Changing the machine or either effect makes a freeze out of date, and so does changing the tempo. The clip gets a mark when that happens. Thaw it or freeze it again."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "When the phone can't keep up"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "If the engine starts falling behind while you play, **the tracks costing the most glow red**, and so does the load meter in the header. Nothing stops."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A track only glows when the engine is running late right then *and* that track is a big part of the load. Freezing it is usually the answer."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Settings · audio** has the details: the worst block, where the time went, and each track's cost."),
        )),
        ManualSection("The editor", "Drawing notes, and what each note can do.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A clip opens with the note grid at the top, the machine's controls in the middle, and the keyboard or pads at the bottom."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The grid"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "For a melodic machine it's a piano roll: pitch up the side, time across."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap an empty square to add a note, tap a note to delete it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Drag a note to move it, or drag its right edge to change its length."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Drag with **two fingers** to scroll and pinch to zoom. One finger always draws."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The **scl** corner changes how the song's scale is shown: all notes, scale notes highlighted, or only the scale notes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "For a drum machine it's a step grid with one row per sound. Tap to add a hit."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Generating"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The die button at the top of the editor writes notes for you. The clip changes as you turn the knobs, so you can listen while the song plays. **OK** keeps the notes and **Cancel** puts the clip back. Undo takes back the whole visit in one step."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rhythm** spreads a number of hits as evenly as it can over a number of steps. **turn** moves the pattern round. A pattern shorter than the bar drifts against it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**line** writes a line in the track's scale, or in the song's key. With neither, it uses a minor pentatonic on the lowest note. **leaps** is how often it jumps instead of moving to a nearby note."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mutate** changes some of the notes already there: some move a step in the scale, some go, a few new ones appear, and velocities shift. **amount** is how much."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "On a drum machine, rhythm and **scatter** work on one sound and leave the others alone. Mutate moves drums in time, not pitch."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**roll** gives a new set of random choices. The same settings and roll always give the same notes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Locking a step"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A lock gives one step its own value for a knob. The snare on step 7 can be tuned higher while every other snare stays where the knob is."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap the diamond (◆) at the top of the editor. It lights, and the grid gets a pink edge."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap the steps to lock: hits in the drum grid, steps in Reflux's step row, notes in the roll. Tap again to let one go."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Turn any knob on the panel below, the machine's or an effect's. The chosen steps get that value; the knob doesn't move for the rest of the clip."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Hold a knob to take the lock off the chosen steps."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Tap the diamond again to go back to drawing notes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A lock lasts one grid step, or the note's length in the roll. Locked steps have a pink diamond in the corner, and a knob with locks on it shows ◆ on its dial."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Turn the knob later without a step chosen and every unlocked step follows it. A knob that already has a drawn lane can't be locked: it shows ∿ and stays put."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Recording"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Press record, then play. What you play goes into the clip, quantised to the clip's grid. You can set a count-in in the settings."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The keyboard is velocity sensitive by where you hit a key: low on the key is soft, high is hard. The button in the bottom bar turns this off so every note is full velocity. The drum pads have their own setting for the same thing."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Drag the row of controls just above the keys up or down to make the keyboard taller or shorter."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Per-note settings"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Below the grid is a lane you can open. Pick what it shows, then drag in it to set that value on each note."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**note volume** - the note's velocity."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**probability** - how often it plays. 50 means half the time."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**trig condition** - when it's allowed to play. `1:4` plays on the first of every four loops. `pre` plays only if the previous conditional note played, `!pr` only if it didn't. `fil` plays only while fill is held, `!fi` only while it isn't."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**ratchet** - how many times the note repeats within its step."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**micro timing** - nudges the note off the grid, up to half a sixteenth either way."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The randomness uses a fixed seed, so you can get the same variation back when you want it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Knobs"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The machine's panel sits between the grid and the keyboard. Inserts, modifiers and sends open the same kind of panel in a window."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Hold a knob to reset it** to where it was when you opened the panel (not to the factory value). Faders and sliders work the same way, including in the mixer."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "While **MIDI mapping** is on, holding a control clears its mapping instead."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Automation"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The strip under the per-note lane records and draws a parameter over time. Turn a knob while recording and the movement is written there. You can also pick a parameter and draw it in by hand."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A knob with a lane in the open clip has a **∿** on its dial. It won't stay where you put it while the clip plays, because the lane moves it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A lane leaves its knob where it finished. When you press play, every automated knob goes back to where it's set in the song first, so the song starts the same every time, and so does an export. In the launcher it doesn't: a clip you launch carries on from wherever the last one left things."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Folding things away"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The lanes, the machine panel and the keyboard each fold away with the small arrow at their edge, which gives the grid more room."),
        )),
        ManualSection("The machines", "Nineteen instruments, a four-track, and what each is for.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Every track has one machine. You pick it when you add the track, and you can change it any time from the name at the top of the editor. **Each machine has its own page below.** This page is for finding the right one."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Bass and lead"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Reflux** - acid bass. One oscillator and a filter that screams. Accent comes from velocity and slide from overlapping notes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Trinity** - the all-round poly synth. Three wavetable oscillators, two filters and a mod matrix."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Ratio** - six-operator FM, with a knob that morphs between two algorithms."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Keys and pads"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Cumulus** - pads built from a spectrum of partials. Smooth and huge."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Manual** - an organ with two manuals, pedals, four models and a rotary cabinet."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Formulate** - an 8-bit chip synth with tracker tables and a waveform you can type in as an equation."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Modelled instruments"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "These model how the real instrument works, so they respond like one."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Filament** - strings you pluck, bow or blow, with a body and sympathetic strings."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Brazen** - brass, from trumpet to tuba."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Timber** - woodwinds: reeds and flutes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Resonance** - eight struck objects that ring into each other."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Drums"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Hexbeat** - synthesized drums in the style of the classic small boxes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Genesis** - the big drum box, with a kick that ducks its own bus."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Forage** - a sample drum machine. Thirteen pads for your own sounds, each with a filter and envelope."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Dice** - a loop slicer with chance on every slice."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Samples and voice"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Pollen** - granular clouds from a file or the live input."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Mosaic** - a multisample player for SoundFonts and your own samples."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Molt** - sing a take in and the piano roll tunes it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Cipher** - a vocoder."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Modular"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Nexus** - a modular synth whose modules are the other machines."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Audio"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Bias** - a four-track for recordings. Four lanes per cell, and a recording made over the song is cut at the scene lines. Its patches are tape types (cassette, reel, telephone and so on) that colour the sound on the way out."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Patches"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Every machine comes with patches, sorted by family. **patch** at the top of the panel opens the browser. Your own patches have their own tab, and they're the only ones you can delete."),
        ), listOf(
            ManualSection("Bias", "A four-track: recordings arranged along the song, four lanes at a time.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Bias is the audio track. Instead of notes, each of its cells plays recordings: four lanes of them at once. You can mute two takes to pick the third, or unmute a second lane to double a vocal."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "How it fits the song"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A Bias cell is a normal clip, so there's no separate timeline. A take recorded across four scenes becomes four cells that all point at the same file, each starting at a different place. Nothing is copied, and it works the same in the arranger and the clip launcher."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A cell lasts its bars times the scene's repeat count and plays straight through, so a scene played twice plays one continuous take."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Recording"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Tap the red dot next to a lane, arm record on the transport, and press play. The song plays while you record, and the other lanes keep playing."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**When you stop, the take is cut at the scene lines**: one cell per scene, and cells are created in scenes where the track was empty. The whole recording also stays in the sound library, so you can undo the split and place it by hand."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "One lane records at a time. If the recorder fell behind and there's a gap in the take, it isn't split (everything after the gap would be misplaced), so it's kept whole instead."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The editor"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Opening a cell shows the four lanes:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "drag a lane's **body** to move where it starts;"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "drag either **end, top half**, to trim it;"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "drag either **end, bottom half**, to fade it in or out. Fading one lane out while another fades in gives you a smooth crossfade;"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "tap the number on the left to **mute** a lane, and the dot below it to arm it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "On a take longer than its cell, both halves of the right edge trim, so you have an end to fade."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Levels, mutes and automation"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Each lane's level and mute are ordinary machine parameters, so you can automate them, map them to a controller and record them."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tempo"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**tempo › takes** chooses whether a take plays at the speed it was recorded at or follows the song's tempo. When it follows, it's time-stretched without changing pitch. When it doesn't, it starts on the bar and plays at its own speed, and the cell turns amber."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Playing a guitar through it"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**monitor**, under **tempo**, feeds the input into this track's output before its effects, so you can hear an amp in the first insert slot while you play. The recording itself stays dry, so you can change the amp later."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Monitor is off by default. Use headphones or an interface, because on the phone speaker it will feed back."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**printed in**, next to it, is for effects you want recorded into the take. They run before the recorder, so they're committed. These are the same two input slots the recording window shows."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Flattening"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**comp** mixes the four lanes (with their levels, mutes and fades) into one file in lane 1. The patch's tape colour isn't baked in. The original recordings stay in the library."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Patches"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Bias's patches are recording media (cassette, reel, telephone, wax cylinder and others). They colour the sound on playback but never change the recordings, so you can try them freely. **Init** plays the file back untouched."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Record first and decide later. The patch, tempo, fades and even the split can all be changed afterwards without touching the file."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Takes can be long. Anything over two minutes is converted once in the background and streamed from storage, so a full-length vocal costs about the same as a short one."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Brazen", "Modelled brass, from trumpet to tuba, or a whole section.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Brazen is a physical model of a brass instrument: lips buzzing against a tube with a bell on the end."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Blowing"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pressure** - how hard you blow. This is the main control, and it's not a volume knob. Too little and nothing sounds; more and the note starts; more again and it gets brighter until it overblows."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**breath** - air noise."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bite** and **tension** - how tight the lips are. Together with pressure they decide which note of the tube you land on, like a real player's embouchure."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**lipdamp** - how damped the lips are."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The tube"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**size** - how long the tube is, from trumpet to tuba."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bore** and **bell** - how the tube flares, which changes how bright it is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**loss** - how much energy is lost in the tube."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**brass** - the blare you get when a brass instrument is played loud."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "A section"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**lock** and **drift** turn one player into several who are nearly together. **growl** and **growlrate** add a growl into the mouthpiece."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Big instruments need more pressure, not less. A tuba is blown hard."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Automate pressure across a note for swells. It changes the tone as well as the level."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Start from a patch and change **size** first."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Cipher", "A vocoder, where you can rearrange which bands drive which.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Cipher is a vocoder. It analyses one sound in frequency bands and puts that shape onto another sound. You can also change which analysis band controls which output band, which is where the unusual sounds come from."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The two sides"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The **modulator** is the sound being analysed, usually a voice from the live input. The **carrier** is the sound being shaped. Cipher has its own carrier built in (an oscillator with **detune**, **pw**, **sub**, **noise** and **cardrive**), so you don't need a second track."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The bands"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bands** - how many. Fewer sounds robotic but clear; more is smoother but less clear."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**low**, **high** and **slope** - the range the bands cover and how they're spread over it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**q** - how narrow each band is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The band map"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Normally band 1 drives band 1. **remap** lets you change that: reverse the order so bright sounds come out dark, spread it wider, **freeze** the current shape while the carrier keeps playing, or **smear** neighbouring bands together."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**role**, **dry** and **wet** set which side is which and how much of each you hear. **unvoiced** handles consonants so the words stay understandable."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**attack**, **release** and **smear** set how quickly each band follows. Fast is clear but can chatter; slow is smooth but can slur."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Modulation"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Two envelopes and two LFOs that can sync to the tempo, into eight matrix rows."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Three sources come from the modulator itself: its **loudness**, **brightness** and **pitch** (pitch needs **track** on). For example, loudness on **smear** makes the words tighten up as they get louder."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The destinations are the map controls (**shift**, **stretch**, **remap**, **freeze**, **smear**, **q** and the band edges) plus the carrier's pitch and mix."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Turn **unvoiced** up until the words are clear, then back off until it stops hissing."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Freeze on a vowel and play chords under it for a vocal pad."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use fewer bands than you'd think. Sixteen usually beats forty for words."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Cumulus", "Pads built from a spectrum of partials.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Cumulus doesn't use normal oscillators. You describe a spectrum (which partials, how loud, how wide) and it builds a wavetable from that, then plays it. It's for pads, drones and huge sounds."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "How it works"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Each partial is given a width instead of being a single frequency. That spread is what makes it lush without needing a chorus."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Building the table takes a moment, so it happens in the background whenever you change the spectrum. Everything from **morph** on is instant."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Spectrum controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bandwidth** and **bwscale** - how wide each partial is, and whether the high ones are wider than the low ones. This is the main control."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tilt** - the balance of bass to treble."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**stretch** - pushes the partials off whole-number multiples, like a piano's top end or a bell. A little goes a long way."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**comb** and **period** - cut regular notches in the spectrum."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**vowel** and its amount - shapes the spectrum towards a vowel."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**odd** - odd against even partials. All odd sounds like a clarinet, all even sounds hollow."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**seed** - the random phases. Change it for a different take on the same sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Playing controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**detune**, **spread** and **width** stack copies. **drift**, **driftrate** and **scatter** keep the sound moving. Then there's a filter with its own envelope, an amp envelope and drive."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use long attacks and releases and let the notes overlap."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Move **morph** while playing rather than the spectrum controls, because morph changes instantly."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Dice", "A loop slicer with chance on every slice.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Dice cuts a loop into slices and plays them back, with a chance of something different happening on each one. It's good for breaks."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Slicing"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**slices** sets how many pieces, and **cut** sets where they fall, from even divisions to the loop's own hits. Each slice has its own **level**, **pan**, **pitch**, **decay** and **dir**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The dice"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Five chances, rolled on every slice:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**swap** - play a different slice."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**reverse** - play it backwards."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**stutter**, with **stutterdiv** - repeat part of it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**drop** - play nothing."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**jump**, with **jumprange** - skip to somewhere else in the loop and carry on."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**seed** decides the rolls and **hold** freezes them. Turn the dice up until you hear something you like, then hold it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Playing"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The slices sit on the grid like drum sounds, so you can reorder the loop by writing a pattern. **rate**, **gate**, **pitch**, **fine** and **accent** control playback, followed by a filter and drive."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Unheld, Dice changes every bar. Held on a good roll, it becomes a part."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "For a break that swings, cut on the hits rather than evenly."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The seed makes it repeatable, so an export sounds like what you arranged."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Filament", "Modelled strings: plucked, picked, struck, bowed or blown.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Filament is a physical model of a string. It doesn't play a recording; it simulates a vibrating string, so it reacts the way a real one does."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The exciter"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**exciter** is what sets the string going: pluck, pick, hammer, bow, breath, or **the live input**, so you can play a string with a microphone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**position** - where on the string it's excited. This changes the tone more than anything else: near the end is thin and nasal, near the middle is round."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hardness** - how sharp the hammer is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**grit** and **length** - how noisy the excitation is and how long it lasts."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The string"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**couple**, **detune** and **spread** - two strings per note, slightly apart, like a twelve-string or a piano's middle register."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**damper** - a felt resting on the string at a position you choose. Good for muted guitar sounds."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The body"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Four resonances stand in for the instrument's body. **size** moves them, **bodydamp** widens them, and **bodymix** sets how much you hear. There are also **sympathetic strings** that ring along with what you play."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Modulation"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Two envelopes and two LFOs that can sync to the tempo, into eight matrix rows (source, destination, depth)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The envelopes do nothing until you route them. One of the sources is **level**, how loudly the string is ringing right now, so you can make the string react to itself."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Destinations include **damping**, **tone**, **position**, **pressure**, **damper**, **tension** and **detune**. **brightness** changes the exciter (grit, or a hammer's hardness), and **rattle** and **volume** are per note. **body**, **drive** and **sympathy** apply to the whole instrument and follow the most recent note."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Change **position** first, before touching the filter or body."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A slow LFO on **position** makes a static pluck come alive."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "If a note rings too long, shorten the decay rather than turning things down."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Forage", "A sample drum machine: thirteen pads for your own sounds.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Forage plays your own samples on thirteen pads, each with some processing to help it fit."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "A pad"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**level**, **pan**, **pitch** and **decay**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**start** and **end** - which part of the file plays. One long recording can feed several pads this way."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mode** - once, loop or hold."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**dir** - forwards or backwards."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**cutoff** with a filter type, and **crush** for bit reduction."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pdecay** - a pitch drop, so a sample can fall like a drum does."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**choke** - pads in the same choke group cut each other off, like an open hat stopping when the closed hat plays."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Slicing one file"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There's a fourteenth slot above the pads for one file shared by the whole machine. Load a drum break into it, ask for thirteen slices, and each pad plays one piece of it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Put your hats in a choke group."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Trim silence off the start of a sample, or it will play late."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Automate **start** across a bar to turn one hit into a stutter."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The sound library won't let you delete a file a track is still using without telling you first."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Formulate", "An 8-bit chip synth, and a waveform you can type in as an equation.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Formulate is a chip synth with pulse, triangle and noise, like the old game consoles. It can also play a waveform you write as a formula."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The chip"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**wave** - pulse, triangle, noise, or the formula."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**duty** - the pulse width. **pwmrate** and **pwmdepth** sweep it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**sub** - adds an octave below."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**noiseshort** - switches the noise between a hiss and a metallic buzz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bits**, **crush** and **smooth** - bit depth, sample rate, and how much the steps are smoothed off."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tracker tables"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There are three, **arp**, **duty** and **vol**, typed in as text and stepped through per tick like a tracker instrument. An arp table of `0 4 7` played fast makes a chord from one voice."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The formula"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**formula** is an expression where **x** goes from 0 to 1 over one cycle of the wave. `sin(x*2*pi)` is a sine, `x*2-1` is a saw, and `(x<0.3)?1:-1` is a pulse with 30% duty."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**formulamode** chooses whether the formula is worked out once per cycle or every sample. The three macro knobs **a**, **b** and **c** can be used by name in the formula, so you can give it controls. If a formula has an error, the panel tells you."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use a macro in the formula and automate it. `sin(x*2*pi*a)` with **a** on a lane sweeps the tone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The tracker tables make a big difference. A plain patch with a good volume table often beats a clever formula without one."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Genesis", "The big drum box, with a bus compressor the kick ducks.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Genesis is the big drum machine, in the style of the boxes that house and techno were made on. Twelve voices, more weight than Hexbeat, and two extras."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Drift"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Real analogue drum machines are never exactly the same twice. **drift** adds a bit of that variation from hit to hit."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The bus compressor"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The whole kit goes through a compressor (**comp**, **compattack**, **comprelease**), and **duck** lets the kick push the rest of the kit down, so the kit pumps with the kick."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The kit"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**kick** - tune, decay, level, **sweep** (the long pitch drop), **punch**, **click** (the attack) and **drive**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**snare**, **clap** (with spread and tone) and **rim**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**toms**, and **hats** with shared tone and tune and separate open and closed decays."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**crash** and **ride**, with a **bell** on the ride."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**cowbell**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Set **duck** before the levels, because it changes how loud everything else sounds."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "If the kick disappears on a phone speaker, add kick **drive** rather than level."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Keep drift low: enough that bars differ, not so much that the hats sound broken."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Hexbeat", "Synthesized drums in the style of the classic small boxes, with a bigger kit.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Hexbeat makes its drums with synthesis rather than samples. It has thirteen voices in the style of the small classic drum machines: punchy, dry and easy to sit in a mix."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The kit"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Kick, rim, snare, clap, three toms, closed and open hat, crash, ride, cowbell and clave."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Every voice has a **level**, and most have **tune** and **decay**, plus a few of their own:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**kick** - tune, decay and **punch** (the pitch drop at the start)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**snare** - tune, decay, tone and **snappy** (drum against noise)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hats** - shared tone and tune, separate closed and open decays."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**clap** - decay, tone, and the spread of the little repeats that make it a clap."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**cymbals** - decay and tone for crash and ride."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bell** and **clave** - tune and level."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**accent** is how much a hard hit adds across the whole kit."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "It opens on the drum grid, which is the best way to edit it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Changing the decays is a quick way to make a pattern tighter or roomier."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Try probability on the hats and a ratchet on the snare so the loop doesn't repeat exactly."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "For drums from your own samples see Forage; for a bigger, heavier kit see Genesis."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Manual", "The organ: two manuals and pedals, four models, and a rotary cabinet.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Manual is an organ with two keyboards and a pedalboard. They all share one generator of 91 tonewheels, like the real thing, so the same wheel plays the fifth of one note and the octave of another. That's why organ chords shimmer and beat slightly."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Drawbars"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Nine per manual, and they're most of the patch. Each one adds one harmonic of the note, as loud as you pull it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The four models"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**model** picks the kind of organ:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**combo** - a transistor organ. Buzzy, with its own attack."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tonewheel** - the classic console organ, with **leakage** (wheels bleeding into each other), **hum** and **age**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**reeds** - a reed organ, with **pressure**, **buzz** and a tremulant."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pipes** - a pipe organ with **principal**, **flute**, **string**, **reed** and **mixture** stops, **chiff** on the attack and **tracker** noise."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Leakage, hum and blower **wind noise** fade in when the organ starts playing and fade out about half a second after it stops, so a track that sits out a scene is silent there."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Key click"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A real tonewheel organ's key contacts close one after another, so each note starts with a little burst of clicks. **click** sets how loud that is, **clickoff** is the click when you let go, and **contacts** spreads the contacts out. With the click off it sounds much less like an organ."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The cabinet"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The rotary speaker is built in: a spinning horn over a drum spinning the other way, each with its own speed and its own ramp up and down, and two microphones you can move. The rotors' position can also be used as a modulation source."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Modulation"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Two envelopes and two LFOs that can sync to the tempo, into eight matrix rows (source, destination, depth)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Four of the sources come from the organ itself: **horn** and **drum** (where the rotors are pointing), the **scanner** from the vibrato, and **wind** (the air pressure). Horn and drum don't move while the rotary is stopped."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The destinations include each drawbar, and all the drawbars of a manual at once."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Switch the rotary speed while playing rather than leaving it on one setting. The speeding up and slowing down is the best part."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Percussion takes over the last drawbar when it's on. That's how the originals worked, and it's usually best left that way."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Molt", "Sing a take in, and the piano roll tunes it.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Molt turns a sung recording into an instrument. It finds the individual pulses of the voice and re-spaces them to change the pitch, so the formants stay where they were and the voice doesn't go chipmunk."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The roll does the tuning"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Draw the notes you want in the piano roll, sing anything, and the take is pulled onto those notes."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tune** - how hard it's pulled. All the way is hard-tune; less is a gentle correction."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rate** - how fast it gets there. Slow sounds like a natural slide, fast sounds stepped."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**robot** - flattens the pitch completely onto the note."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The voice"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**formant** - moves the formants without changing the pitch, so the singer sounds bigger or smaller."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mega** - a megaphone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**start** and **loop** - which part of the take plays, and whether it loops."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Then a filter, an amp envelope and drive."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Keep low rumble out of the recording. Handling noise from holding the phone can throw the pitch detection off. The recorder's low cut helps, and it's on by default for Molt."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Consonants are copied rather than pitched, so they stay crisp."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Singing roughly and letting the roll fix it is a perfectly good way to work."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Mosaic", "A multisample player: zones across the keyboard and velocity, plus grain clouds.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Mosaic plays maps of samples. Each sample (a zone) covers a range of keys and velocities, so together they make a playable instrument."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The map"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A **zone** is a file, a key range, a velocity range, a root note and a tuning. Build zones by hand from your own recordings, or load a **SoundFont** to get a whole map at once."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**keyfade** and **velfade** - crossfades between neighbouring zones so you don't hear the joins."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**scan** and **scanamt** - move smoothly through the velocity layers with a knob instead of velocity. Put scan on the mod wheel to blend a soft and a loud sample."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Playing"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**start**, **loop**, **reverse**, **envfrom** and **filemod** decide how each file is played. After that there's a filter with its own envelope and key tracking, two envelopes, two LFOs, glide and voice modes."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Grains"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**grain** plays the map as a grain cloud instead, with **gsize**, **gdensity**, **gpos**, **gspray**, **gpitch** and **grate**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Set the crossfades before judging how a map sounds."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Check the root notes. A wrong root puts everything out of tune."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Big maps use a lot of memory. The sound library tells you what a song is using before you delete anything."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Nexus", "A modular synth whose modules are the other machines.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Nexus lets you build your own machine. Its modules are the app's own instruments, so you can wire a Reflux filter, a Trinity oscillator and a Resonance mode bank together."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The patch is text"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A Nexus patch is a list of modules and connections written out as text, so you can read it, copy it and send it to someone. It has its own screen, because a patch needs room."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Its knobs are normal parameters, so anything in a patch can be automated, mapped to a controller and recorded."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Macros"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Eight macros, **macro1** to **macro8**, plus **morph**. The patch decides what they control, so a big patch can be played with a few knobs."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Audio input"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There's an audio input module, so anything coming into the phone can be run through a patch."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Keep patches small and name your macros."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Wire **morph** to the two or three things that change the patch the most."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Nexus isn't the easiest place to start. The other machines do most things more directly; Nexus is for combinations they don't cover."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Pollen", "Granular clouds from a file or the live input, whose grains can spawn more grains.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Pollen plays a sound as a cloud of short grains. The source is either a file you load or the live input, recorded into a loop as you play."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The cloud"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**size** and **sizespread** - how long each grain is, and how much that varies."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**density** and **jitter** - how many grains per second, and how irregular."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**window** and **skew** - the shape of each grain's fade in and out."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**position**, **scan** and **spray** - where grains come from in the buffer, whether that point moves, and how far they scatter around it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**snap** - lines grains up with the hits in the source, so rhythmic material stays rhythmic."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**panspread** and **width**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Live input"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**source** switches to the input. **buffer** sets how much is kept, **freeze** holds it, and **capture** grabs what's in it. A live buffer **isn't saved with the song and is silent in an export**, and the panel reminds you of that."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Pollination"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**bloom** and **generations**: grains can spawn more grains at a related position and pitch, up to the depth you set. A little thickens the sound; a lot turns one note into an evolving texture. **drift** and **mutate** set how far the new grains wander."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Density and size work against each other: long grains at high density make a wall, short ones make a texture."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use **snap** on anything with a beat."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The pitch scatter can be locked to a scale so the cloud stays in key."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Under lean quality (Settings) the cloud uses half as many grains."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Ratio", "Six-operator FM, with a knob that morphs between two algorithms.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Ratio is the FM synth. It has six operators, each a sine wave that can be a carrier (you hear it) or a modulator (it changes the sound of another). How they're connected is the algorithm."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Morphing algorithms"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Instead of picking one algorithm, you pick two, `algoa` and `algob`, and **morph** blends between them. The in-between settings aren't on any standard algorithm list, and you can automate the morph. A pad can open up from two operators to six over a few bars."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The operators"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Each of the six has:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**ratio** - its frequency as a multiple of the note. **fixed** unlinks it from the note so it stays at one frequency, which is good for formants."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**level** - for a modulator, how much FM it adds. For a carrier, how loud it is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**fb** - feedback into itself. Turn it up to get from a sine towards a saw."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**A D S R** - its own envelope. On a modulator this shapes the tone, not the volume."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**key** tracking, **fine** tune, **pan** and **mode**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Everything else"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A filter with its own envelope, three extra envelopes for modulation, three LFOs that can sync to the tempo, and ten matrix rows (source, second source, destination, depth) that work like Trinity's."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**snap** keeps ratios on useful values (whole numbers, odd numbers, semitones, bell partials), and **skew** bends them all away from those values together."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Whole-number ratios (1, 2, 3) sound harmonic. Others (1.41, 3.14) sound like bells and metal. Good patches often mix both."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Give modulators shorter envelopes than carriers. A bright attack decaying into a soft body is the classic electric piano."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Feedback on the top operator of a stack is the cheapest way to add brightness."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Reflux", "Acid bass: one oscillator, one filter that screams, and lines you play rather than program.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Reflux is one oscillator, one filter and one envelope, set up so you can play a bassline in rather than build it step by step."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Accent and slide"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Classic acid basslines come from two things: accent and slide. Reflux maps them to things you already do when playing:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Accent is velocity.** Play a note harder and it's accented: louder, brighter, and with more filter envelope."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Slide is legato.** Overlap two notes and the second one glides from the first instead of retriggering. Leave a gap and it plucks."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "So you can play a line on the keyboard, or draw it in the roll with overlapping notes for the slides."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**osc** - the waveform, saw or square, plus a sub oscillator an octave down."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**filter** - cutoff and resonance, plus envelope amount and decay. This is where most of the sound is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**env** - one decay envelope and how much of it goes to the filter. Short decays are plucky, long ones are rubbery."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**accent** - how much an accented note adds to level and filter. At zero, velocity does nothing."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**glide** - how long a slide takes."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**drive** - the output stage, where the scream comes from."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Resonance and drive together make the classic sound. Most acid patches have both up and the cutoff moving."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Record the cutoff knob as automation while the song plays rather than drawing it in."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Two Refluxes playing the same clip, one plucked and one sliding, panned apart with slightly different cutoffs, is an old trick that still works."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "*Reflux was called Subvert in earlier versions. Songs and patches from then open as Reflux.*"),
            )),
            ManualSection("Resonance", "Eight struck objects that ring into each other.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Resonance is modal percussion. Each of its eight pads is an object (a drum, a bar, a bell and so on) that rings at a set of frequencies, and playing a pad strikes it. Nothing is sampled."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "A pad"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**modes** - how many partials it has. Few sounds like a woodblock, many like a bell."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**shape** - membrane, bar, plate, tube, bowl or metal. This sets how the partials are spaced, which is most of what makes a drum sound different from a bar."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**inharm** - stretches the spacing."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**decay** and **damp** - how long it rings, and whether the highs die first."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hard** - how hard the beater is (how much top end)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hit** - where you strike it. Striking in a different place brings some partials out and hides others, so it changes the sound a lot."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bend** and **bendtime** - a drop in pitch after the hit, like a tom."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**drive**, **pan**, **level** and **couple**."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Coupling"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**coupling** sets how much the pads ring into each other. Hit one and the others answer quietly at their own pitches, so a kit sounds like objects in one room. **humanise** varies each hit a little."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Set **hit** and **shape** before tuning."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Go easy on coupling. A little sounds like a room; a lot feeds back."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "With long decays you can play melodies on the pads."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Timber", "Modelled woodwinds: reeds and flutes.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Timber is the woodwind version of Brazen. A woodwind is either a reed vibrating against a mouthpiece or a jet of air blown across an edge, feeding a tube with holes in it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The family"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**family** picks the kind of instrument:"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**reed** - clarinet and saxophone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**double reed** - oboe and bassoon."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**air jet** - flute. **aim** sets how the air hits the edge, like a flute player's lips."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The tube"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bore** - cylindrical or conical. A cylinder gives the hollow clarinet sound; a cone sounds more like a sax."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**body** and **bell** - the shape of the end."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**loss** - how much energy is lost in the tube."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**fingering**, **below** and **answer** - the tone holes, including the part of the tube past the note you're playing."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**fork** - cross-fingerings, which sound slightly different from normal fingerings, as on a real instrument."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Blowing"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**pressure** and **breath** work as on Brazen. The flute family adds **jet** and **aim**, and **tongue** adds articulation."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Pressure decides the register. Overblow a clarinet and it jumps up a twelfth rather than an octave, like the real one."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The breath noise is a big part of the realism."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use **tongue** between notes so a phrase doesn't sound like one long note."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Trinity", "Three oscillators, wavetables, two filters and a mod matrix: the all-rounder.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Trinity is the general-purpose poly synth: three oscillators into two filters, with a modulation matrix on top."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Oscillators"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Each oscillator plays a wavetable: a row of waveshapes that **pos** moves through. Automate **pos** to sweep the tone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**density** stacks copies of the oscillator, **detune** spreads them out, and **drift** makes each copy wander a little. This is how you get a big supersaw-style sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hard** is oscillator sync and **pw** is pulse width."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**fm21** and **fm32** are FM between the oscillators (2 into 1, 3 into 2), for metallic and bell-like sounds."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**noise** and its **colour** are for breath, snare tails and so on."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Filters"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There are two. **route** puts them in series, in parallel, or split with **balance** between them. Each has its own type, drive, key tracking and envelope amount."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Modulation"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Six envelopes and three LFOs feed twelve matrix rows."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The **a** envelope is the amplitude and **f** is the filter. The other four do nothing until you route them. Each LFO has a wave, a rate that can sync to the tempo, delay, phase, key sync, one-shot and slew."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A matrix row has a source, a second source, a destination and a depth. The second source scales the first. For example, src = an envelope and src2 = mod wheel gives you an envelope you can fade in with the wheel."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Start with the oscillators (table position and density) rather than the filter. That's where Trinity's character is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "High density costs the most CPU. If a song is struggling, freeze the Trinity clip with the biggest stack."),
            )),
        )),
        ManualSection("Effects and mixing", "Two inserts a track, two sends, groups, and the master.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Inserts"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Each track has two insert effect slots. **fx** in the editor's bottom bar shows them in place of the machine panel."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There are sixteen effects. Each one has the usual controls plus one extra, shown in the accent colour. Every effect ends with **gain**, an output level trim, because turning up the wet/dry mix can change the level."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Each effect has its own page below.**"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Time"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Delay** - echoes on a note value, and it can duck while you play."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Reverb** - a room that can also freeze, gate, shimmer, or crush itself down to 8 bits."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tone"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Eq** - three bands and a tilt."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Filter** - low, band or high pass, moved by an LFO, the signal's level, or another track."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Width** - wider, narrower, or mono below a frequency."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Drive"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Distortion** - four kinds of clipping and a bias control."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Amp** - a guitar amp with a cabinet you can resize."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Bitcrusher** - fewer bits and a lower sample rate, with an unsteady clock if you want it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Level"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Compressor** - the usual controls, a sidechain from any track, and a tempo-synced pump."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Gate** - a noise gate that can be opened by another track."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Movement"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Chorus** - two to four detuned voices that drift."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Flanger** - a short sweeping delay, with negative feedback for the hollow sound."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Phaser** - two to eight stages."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Tremolo** - volume on an LFO, or auto-pan."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Pitch"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Shifter** - frequency shifting, for metallic and detuned sounds."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Harmonizer** - adds two voices at scale steps so the harmony stays in key."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Input effects"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There are two more effect slots on the **input**. They're at the top of the recording window's **record** page (under **printed into the recording**), and on Bias's panel under **printed in**."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "An effect **on the input** runs before anything else hears the audio, so it's **recorded into the take**."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "An effect **on a track** runs on playback, so you can change it later and the recording stays untouched."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "So put an amp on the input if you've decided on the sound, or on the track if you want to keep your options open. Input effects keep their **mix** control, unlike sends."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The mixer"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The mixer button opens a strip for each track, plus the master. The tabs down its left edge switch between the mixer and the three perform pages (see **Perform** below)."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Fader and meter** for each track, with mute and solo."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Two send amounts** per track, going to two effects shared by the whole song."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Pan**, and a MIDI row for what the track sends out."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A **∿ next to a track's name** means something on that channel is automated, which is why the control won't stay where you put it. Tap it to see which lanes, and to clear them from every clip on the track. Notes aren't touched."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "After the tracks come the **groups**, if the song has any, and a **+ group** button. Last is the master strip, which has the master fader, the limiter's **limit drive**, and the loudness readout. Under that is a grid of buttons: the two sends on top, the two master inserts (**fx1**, **fx2**) in the middle, and the limiter (**lim**) and the click (**♩**) at the bottom. Tap one to turn it on or off, and hold a send or insert to choose its effect and set it up."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Perform"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The tabs down the mixer's left edge are **mix**, **hold**, **pad** and **live**. The last three are for playing the song rather than mixing it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The effects on **hold** and **pad** work on the whole mix, after the master inserts. They're only on while you hold them, unless **latch** is on. With latch on, a tap turns something on and another tap turns it off, and the pad stays where you leave it. Turning latch off lets go of everything on that page."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "If the song has groups, **on all** picks what the effects work on: the whole mix, or just one group, e.g. repeat only the drums. Tap it to go through the groups."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "If you're recording, everything you do on **hold** and **pad** is recorded into the clip of the last track you opened, as automation, and the song plays it back the same way. Stopping the song lets go of anything held."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "### Hold"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**repeat** loops the last slice of the song. The five buttons are the slice length, from a beat down to a sixteenth. Slide along them without letting go to change the length. While the song is playing the slice starts on the beat, so it stays in time."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**gate** chops the sound on and off in time. The five buttons are how fast: eighths, sixteenths, thirty-seconds, or eighth and sixteenth triplets. Slide along them the same way as repeat."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**reverse** plays the last beat backwards, over and over, in time with the song. Hold it with a repeat and the repeat plays backwards."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**stop** slows the song down to a stop like a tape machine losing power. Let go and it spins back up. **stop** under it sets how long it takes."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**riser** builds up to a drop. A high pass climbs and noise rises under it over 1, 2 or 4 bars (**riser** under it), then snaps back when you let go."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "### Pad"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Left and right is a filter: low pass to the left of the middle, high pass to the right. Up is how much of the mix goes into an echo. Let go and the echo keeps repeating and dies away. **echo** sets its time and **feedback** how long it lasts."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**x** and **y** change what the pad does:"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**x crush** makes left and right a bit crusher instead of a filter: fewer samples to the left of the middle, fewer bits to the right. It crushes a quiet mix as much as a loud one."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**y wash** throws into a short smeared echo that sounds more like a big reverb than repeats. It has no time setting; **feedback** still sets how long it lasts."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Under the pad, **kill low**, **kill mid** and **kill high** take that part of the sound out while you hold them: below 250 Hz, 250 Hz to 2.5 kHz, and above 2.5 kHz."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "### Live"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A mute button for every track (the same mute as the mixer's), and **fill**."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "While the song plays, a mute waits for the next bar and lands on it, so tracks drop in and out in time. The button is outlined until it does. **mute on** sets what it waits for: a bar, a beat, or nothing (**now**). When the song is stopped, mutes happen straight away. If you're recording, the mutes are recorded into each track's own clip."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Notes set to play on fill only play while **fill** is held. Fill isn't recorded."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Master inserts"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**fx1** and **fx2** process the whole mix, including the sends, before the master fader and the limiter. Use them for things that apply to the whole song: a gentle EQ, a bit of glue compression, some tape-style drive, or narrowing the low end."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The difference from a send: a send is added alongside the mix, and each track chooses how much to send. A master insert processes everything equally. So reverb and delay usually go on sends, and EQ and compression for the whole song go on the master inserts."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Groups"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A group is a strip in the mixer that tracks can be routed through. It isn't a track: it has no machine and no clips, and it doesn't appear in the song grid."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Tap **+ group** in the mixer to add one (up to four). Tap a group's name to rename it. The **✕** beside it deletes the group, and so does holding the name. Once the song has a group, each track strip gets an extra row at the bottom: tap it to send that track to the master or to one of the groups."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A group strip has a fader, pan, mute and solo, and lists the tracks going into it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Everything routed into a group goes through the group's two effects (**fx1**, **fx2**) and its fader before the master. For example, put one compressor on all the drums, or turn a whole section down with one fader."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A track's **sends** still go straight to the send effects, not through the group."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Solo** a group to hear all of it. Solo a track inside a group to hear just that track, still through the group's effects."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Deleting a group sends its tracks back to the master."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "When you export **stems**, each group is a stem with its tracks in it, and those tracks don't get their own stems."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Sidechain"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The compressor, gate and filter can react to another track instead of their own input. Set **sidechain** in the effect to the track you want. The classic use is a compressor on the bass keyed to the kick, so the bass ducks on every kick."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The sidechain hears the other track before its fader and mute, so turning the kick down doesn't weaken the ducking, and a muted kick still works as a trigger."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The two sends"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "They start as a reverb and a delay, but either can hold any effect. The send buttons on the master strip show what's on them. Tap to turn one on or off, and hold to choose and set up the effect. The send sliders on each track are named after what's on the sends."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A send is always fully wet: only the effect comes back, because the dry sound is already in the mix. That's why a send's editor has no mix control."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Some effects are fun on a send: a pitch shifter fed a little from several tracks, or a bitcrusher for a trashed copy of the mix sitting behind it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tempo and song settings"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Tap the tempo in the song header to open the song's settings."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**beats a minute** - type a number or use the buttons either side. **tap** sets it from four taps."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bar** - the time signature, from 4/4 to 7/8. A scene can have its own."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**swing** - how late the offbeats are. **triplet** is the classic shuffle. **swing on** chooses whether it swings sixteenths or eighths."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**key** - the song's key and scale. The piano roll shades notes outside it, and a new track gets a matching scale. It doesn't change any notes, and doesn't override a track with its own scale set."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The **click** and **link** pages are in the same window."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "How swing works"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Swing moves the offbeats later without changing the order of notes, so a loosely played part swings along with everything else."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A **track can have its own swing**, e.g. swung drums over a straight bass."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**What you record is stored straight.** When you play against a swung song, the swing is taken back out before the notes are written. The roll shows where you meant the notes, playback puts them where you played them, and if you turn the swing down later the part is straight rather than lopsided."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The **click is never swung**."),
        ), listOf(
            ManualSection("Amp", "A guitar amp with a cabinet you can resize.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A guitar amp modelled as the full chain: a preamp, a tone stack, a power stage and a speaker cabinet. The tone stack sits between the preamp and the power stage, like on a real amp."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**drive** and **bias** - the preamp. Bias makes the clipping lopsided for an older, woollier sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bass**, **mid**, **treble** and **stack** - these interact like a real amp's tone controls: bass and treble up scoops the mids. **stack** (us, uk or modern) changes the whole character of the amp, not just the tone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**presence** - pushes the power stage harder in the upper mids."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**master** - how hard the power stage is driven. Low drive with high master sounds different from the other way round at the same volume."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**sag** - how much the power supply droops when you play hard, which makes the amp feel springy."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**cab** - the cabinet, or off."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**size** and **cone** *(extra)* - resize the cabinet smoothly, from a small combo to a 4x12 to a big bass cab, and anything in between."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mic**, **edge** and **room** - where the microphone is: angle, centre of the cone or edge, and distance."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Set **drive** and **master** before the tone controls."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "If it sounds harsh, move the mic with **edge** before turning down treble."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Keep **mix** low on a bass or synth for some grit without losing the low end."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Amp is the most CPU-hungry effect in the app. One is fine; a dozen is a lot."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Bitcrusher", "Fewer bits and a lower sample rate, with an unsteady clock if you want it.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**bits** lowers the resolution and **rate** lowers the sample rate. They sound quite different, so they have separate controls."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bits** - 16 down to 1. Adds noise that follows the signal."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rate** - 48 kHz down to 500 Hz. This gives the metallic, lo-fi sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**jitter** *(extra)* - makes the sample clock unsteady, like old samplers."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tone** *(extra)* - a low-pass after the crushing to take off some of the fizz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Most of the lo-fi sound comes from **rate**. **bits** on its own mostly adds hiss."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A little **jitter** makes it sound more like an old machine."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "On a send with **tone** around 4 kHz, you get a trashed copy of the song behind the clean one."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Chorus", "Two to four detuned voices that drift.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A chorus made of several voices at different delays, each swept by its own part of the LFO, so it sounds like a group rather than one doubled copy."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rate** - how fast the voices sweep, as a note value."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**depth** - how far each one moves. Small thickens; large gets seasick."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**voices** - two, three or four. Two sounds like doubling, four like an ensemble."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**spread** - how wide the voices are in stereo."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**drift** *(extra)* - each voice wanders randomly, like an old analogue chorus with an unsteady clock."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "If it sounds fake, use more voices and less depth."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A little **drift** makes it sound analogue."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "It's wide by default. On a lead or bass, turn **spread** down."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Compressor", "The usual controls, a sidechain from any track, and a tempo-synced pump.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**threshold** - the level it starts working above, -60 to 0 dB."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**ratio** - how hard it compresses, 1:1 to 20:1. Above about 10:1 it acts as a limiter."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**attack** - how fast it reacts, 0.1 to 100 ms. Slower lets the start of each hit through, which makes drums punchier."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**release** - how fast it lets go, 10 to 1000 ms."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**makeup** - adds level back, up to 24 dB."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pump** *(extra)* - ducks the sound in time with the song, like a sidechain from a kick that isn't there."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pumprate** *(extra)* - how often it pumps: 1/16, 1/8, 1/4 or 1/2."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**sidechain** - what the compressor listens to: **own** (this track), or another track. With another track, this one gets pushed down whenever that track is loud. It hears the other track before its fader and mute, so a muted kick still works as a trigger."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "On drums, **attack** changes the tone: around 20 ms is punchier, 1 ms is flatter."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Duck the bass under the kick**: on the bass, set **sidechain** to the drums, ratio 8:1 or more, the fastest attack, release 80-150 ms, and lower the threshold until it ducks 6-10 dB on each kick."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A real sidechain follows the actual drums, fills included. **pump** doesn't need routing and never misses a beat, which is handy before the drums are written."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "On the master, go gentle: 2:1, with 2-3 dB of reduction on the loud parts."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Delay", "Echoes on a note value, and it can duck while you play.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A delay timed to the song. **time** is a note value, so a dotted-eighth delay stays a dotted eighth when the tempo changes."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**time** - the gap between echoes, from 1/32 to a whole bar, including dotted and triplet values."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**feedback** - how much of each echo is fed back in, up to 0.95."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tone** - a low-pass on the echoes, so each repeat is duller than the last."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pingpong** - how much the echoes bounce between left and right."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - how much of the echo you hear."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**duck** *(extra)* - turns the echoes down while the dry sound is playing and brings them back in the gaps."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**wobble** *(extra)* - makes the delay time drift slowly like a tape machine. A little warms it up; more detunes the echoes."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Bring **tone** down to a few kHz so long feedback fades into the background instead of piling up."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use **duck** rather than lowering the mix to stop the delay swamping a part."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "On a send the delay is always fully wet, and each track's send slider sets how much goes to it."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Distortion", "Four kinds of clipping and a bias control.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Drive into clipping, with a tone control after it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**drive** - how hard the signal is pushed, 1 to 40."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tone** - a low-pass after the clipping, to tame the fizz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry. A little distortion mixed under the clean sound thickens it without making it dirty."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mode** *(extra)* - soft, hard, fold or diode. Soft rounds the peaks, hard squares them off, fold makes the sound change character as it gets louder, and diode is lopsided."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bias** *(extra)* - makes the top and bottom of the wave clip differently. This adds the warmer, tube-like harmonics."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A little bias is what makes distortion sound \"warm\"."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Fold changes the tone as you play harder rather than just getting louder."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "This effect isn't oversampled (unlike Amp), so it has some rough edges on purpose."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Eq", "Three bands and a tilt.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A low shelf, a sweepable mid band and a high shelf."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**lowgain** and **lowfreq** - a shelf up to 15 dB either way, from 40 to 500 Hz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**midgain**, **midfreq** and **midq** - the mid band. **midq** goes from broad (0.3) to very narrow (6)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**highgain** and **highfreq** - the high shelf, from 1.5 to 16 kHz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tilt** *(extra)* - one knob that lifts the top and lowers the bottom, or the other way round."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**gain** - output level."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Cutting usually helps more than boosting."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Use a narrow Q to find and cut a ringing frequency (boost, sweep until it's worst, then cut). Use a broad Q and small moves to change the tone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Try **tilt** first when something sounds too dark or too thin."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Filter", "Low, band or high pass, moved by an LFO, the signal's level, or another track.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A resonant filter that can move on its own, in time with the song or with the sound going through it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**cutoff** - 20 Hz to 20 kHz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**reso** - resonance, up to the edge of self-oscillation."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mode** - low pass, band pass or high pass."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**lforate** *(extra)* - the LFO speed as a note value, so it stays in time."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**lfodepth** *(extra)* - how far the LFO moves the cutoff. Negative sweeps down instead of up."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**envdepth** *(extra)* - how much the signal's level moves the cutoff. Positive opens it when you play harder (auto-wah); negative closes it."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**sidechain** - whose level moves it: **own**, or another track. With a negative **envdepth** and the kick as the source, the filter closes on every kick and opens again after."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A negative depth lets you leave the filter open and have it dip, which sounds quite different from a sweep up."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Combine a slow LFO with a little envelope so it moves with the song and with what's played."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Resonance and band pass lose level. Use **gain** to make it up."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Flanger", "A short sweeping delay, with negative feedback for the hollow sound.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "One very short delay, swept and mixed back with the dry sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rate** - the sweep speed, as a note value."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**depth** - how far the sweep travels."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**feedback** - up to 0.95. Low is a gentle whoosh, high is the jet plane."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**negative** *(extra)* - flips the feedback, for a hollow sound instead of a ringing one."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**spread** - how far out of step the two channels sweep."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry. Half is the strongest effect."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Keep the mix around half. Fully wet loses most of the effect."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Try **negative** on sustained chords."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A slow rate and small depth on a send adds subtle movement to a mix."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Gate", "A noise gate, with a filter on what it listens to, and a sidechain.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**threshold** - the level it opens at, -80 to 0 dB."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hyst** - how far below the threshold the signal has to drop before it closes, up to 24 dB. This stops it flickering on a note that sits near the threshold."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**attack** - how fast it opens, 0.05 to 50 ms."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**hold** - how long it stays open after the signal drops, up to 500 ms."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**release** - how fast it closes once the hold is over."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**duck** *(extra)* - how far down \"closed\" is. All the way is a full gate; around -12 dB suits drums."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**key** *(extra)* - a high-pass filter on what the gate listens to (not on the sound). Raise it so the gate ignores hum and rumble but still opens for the notes."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**sidechain** - what opens it: **own**, or another track. The **key** filter still applies."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "There's no mix control, since a half-open gate would just be the noise at half level."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "If it chatters, make **hold** longer before touching release."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "For a guitar with mains hum, set **key** to about 120 Hz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Key a sustained pad's gate to the hi-hat track to chop it into a rhythm. Set **duck** to about -12 dB so the gaps aren't silent."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Before an amp it removes the pickup's hiss; after the amp it removes the amp's. On an input slot it cleans up the recording itself."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Harmonizer", "Adds two voices at scale steps, so the harmony stays in key.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A pitch shifter that knows the key. You set the intervals in scale steps rather than semitones, so a third comes out major or minor depending on the note, like a real harmony part."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**interval** and **interval2** - the two added voices, -7 to +7 scale steps."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**scale** *(extra)* - which scale the steps are counted in. The same list as the Scale modifier."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**key** *(extra)* - the key."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**window** - 10 to 120 ms. Short follows fast parts but sounds grainier; long is smoother but blurs attacks."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**feedback** - feeds the output back in, stacking the interval on itself."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Set the key, or it's just a pitch shifter and some thirds will be wrong."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "On drums or anything percussive, use a short window (around 20 ms)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A fifth or an octave below is often more useful than a third above, and exposes tuning less."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Two steps with feedback stacks up a chord."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Phaser", "Two to eight stages.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Sweeping notches from a chain of allpass filters. Unlike a flanger's, the notches aren't evenly spaced, which gives a phaser its own sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rate** - the sweep speed, as a note value."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**depth** - how far the notches travel."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**feedback** - up to 0.9. Makes the notches ring."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**stages** - two, four, six or eight. More stages means more notches and a stronger effect."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**spread** *(extra)* - sweeps the two channels out of step, so it's wide. Great on pads."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Change **stages** first to set the character. Four is the classic pedal."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "More feedback makes the sweep more obvious."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A slow phaser on a send adds movement without being noticeable."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Reverb", "A room that can also freeze, gate, shimmer, or crush itself.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A reverb with the usual size, damping and predelay, plus some extras."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**size** - how big the space is."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**damp** - how absorbent the room is. Damped sounds like carpet, undamped like tiles."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**tone** - a low-pass on the reverb tail."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**predelay** - a gap before the reverb starts, up to 200 ms."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**freeze** *(extra)* - holds the tail forever."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**gate** *(extra)* - cuts the tail off sharply, for the classic 80s gated snare."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**shimmer** *(extra)* - feeds the tail back an octave up, so a held chord keeps rising."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**bits** and **crush** *(extra)* - bit reduction and sample-rate reduction on the tail only."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**wobble** *(extra)* - makes the tail drift slightly so it's never quite still."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "If the reverb makes things muddy, try 30-60 ms of **predelay** before making it smaller."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Shimmer works best with a low mix and a long tail."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Put **bits** and **crush** on a send for a lo-fi copy of the mix behind the clean one."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Shifter", "Frequency shifting, for metallic and detuned sounds.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "This isn't a pitch shifter. A pitch shifter keeps the sound's harmonics in proportion. A frequency shifter moves every harmonic up or down by the same number of hertz, so the sound stops being in tune with itself. That's what makes it useful."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**shift** - -500 to +500 Hz. Small amounts are the most useful."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**fine** - ±20 Hz, for tiny shifts that just beat slowly."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**spread** *(extra)* - shifts the two channels in opposite directions. A couple of hertz makes a mono sound wide and slowly swirling."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**feedback** - feeds the output back in so it shifts again each pass, for metallic cascades."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Under 1 Hz it acts like a very slow, wide modulation. Lovely on pads."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "A few hertz or more makes held notes sound inharmonic: great on drums, bells and noise, usually wrong on chords."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Feedback with a small shift turns a hit into a rising metallic ladder."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Tremolo", "Volume on an LFO, or auto-pan.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rate** - the pulse speed, as a note value."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**depth** - how deep each dip goes. At full, the sound cuts out between pulses."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**shape** - sine, triangle or square. Square gives a chopped sound."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**pan** *(extra)* - moves the two channels out of step. At zero it's a tremolo, at full it's an auto-pan, and in between it wobbles around."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**skew** *(extra)* - changes the shape of each pulse: a stab (fast down, slow up) or a swell (slow down, fast up)."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**mix** - wet against dry. On a tremolo this effectively sets the depth."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Square shape with **skew** hard over at a sixteenth rate turns a held chord into a rhythm."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Try **pan** around a third of the way for movement that never sits clearly on one side."),
            )),
            ManualSection("Width", "Wider, narrower, mono below a frequency, or rotated.", listOf(
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Controls the stereo image."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The controls"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**width** - 0 is mono, 1 is unchanged, 2 is twice as wide. Anything in the centre stays in the centre."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**below** *(extra)* - makes everything under this frequency mono, from 20 to 500 Hz."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**haas** *(extra)* - delays one side by up to 20 ms. A few milliseconds makes a sound wider without changing its tone."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**rotate** - turns the whole image left or right without changing its width."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Tips"),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "Set **below** to around 120 Hz on most things. Bass doesn't need stereo, and stereo bass can vanish on mono speakers."),
                ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**haas** holds up better than **width** when the mix is played in mono."),
            )),
        )),
        ManualSection("Modifiers", "Scale, chord and arp, applied to notes as you play them in.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Every track has three modifiers, in the row just above the keyboard. **Tap one to turn it on or off, and hold it to open its settings.**"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A modifier changes what you play before it's written down. Play one key with the chord modifier on and you hear three notes, and if you're recording, the clip gets three notes. Notes from a MIDI keyboard or controller go through the modifiers too."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**The clip is what you hear.** Clips play straight to the machine without going through the modifiers, so the piano roll always shows exactly what plays, and you can edit the notes afterwards."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**They don't change what's already recorded.** Turning the arp off doesn't un-arpeggiate a part you played with it on. If you want it different, undo and play it again."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Scale"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Thirty-three scales, in any key. With a scale on, the keyboard only shows notes in the scale, so you get more range on a small screen and can't hit a wrong note. MIDI notes outside the scale are moved to the nearest one that fits."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The **scl** corner of the piano roll chooses how the scale is shown: all notes, scale notes highlighted, or only scale notes. On a track without its own scale, the roll uses the song's key (set behind the tempo button)."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Chord"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Turns every note into a chord. Either a fixed chord shape, or the chord the current scale builds on that note, so the harmony stays in key. **strum** spreads the notes out in time, and recordings keep the strum."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Arpeggio"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Plays the notes you're holding one at a time. You choose the order, speed, octave range, note length, and a chance for each step to be skipped."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Recording with the arp on writes out every note it plays, so a held chord becomes a line you can edit afterwards."),
        )),
        ManualSection("MIDI and playing with others", "Keyboards, controller mapping, clock, MPE and Link.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Everything here is under **MIDI…** in the file menu."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Playing from a keyboard"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "USB and Bluetooth MIDI keyboards work directly. Notes go either to whichever track is open, or to a track you pin so it stays the same whatever you're looking at."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**MPE** works on the machines that support it: bending one finger bends only that note. You set the zone and bend range here. Per-note bend, pressure and slide are recorded too."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Mapping a controller"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Long-press redo to enter mapping mode. Controls that can be mapped are highlighted. Tap one, then move a knob or press a key on your controller to link them. Knobs, faders, mixer controls and transport buttons can all be mapped, to a CC or a note."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "A mapped knob records into automation just like moving it by hand. Mapped buttons like play, stop and fill don't record."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Clock"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The app can send MIDI clock (with start, stop and song position) to hardware, and it can follow an incoming clock. When following, the tempo comes from the other device."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**follow** on the MIDI window's **control** tab has three settings:"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**on** always follows, even with nothing coming in."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**auto** follows a clock when one arrives and goes back to the song's own tempo a second after it stops."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**off** ignores incoming clock."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Each track chooses what it sends out: notes, clock, or both."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Link"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Ableton Link shares tempo and bar position with other Link apps on the same network, both ways. Turn it on and the number of connected apps appears. It keeps everyone's downbeat together without anyone being in charge."),
        )),
        ManualSection("Recording and samples", "Getting audio in, and what to do with it.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "The recording window"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "One window handles recording, and it opens wherever a machine needs audio: a pad on Forage, a loop for Dice, a buffer for Pollen, or a take for Molt."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Record** - choose the input, watch the level, and record. - **source** is **in** for the microphone or what's plugged in, or **resample** to record what the app is playing. - The **tuner** in the input card shows the nearest note and how many cents off you are, and turns green within four cents. It listens before the input effects, and shows nothing unless it's sure of the note. - **printed into the take** holds two effects that are recorded into the file, e.g. a guitar amp. Effects on a *track* can be changed any time instead."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Edit** - trim the ends, set the level, and cut low rumble. **norm** and **rev** at the top normalise and reverse the whole file."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Library** - everything you've recorded or imported."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The result is a file, so the same recording can be used by more than one machine."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Importing"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "You can import WAV, AIFF, FLAC and MP3. Everything is converted to WAV once on the way in, so songs load quickly."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Recording onto a track"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Bias** is the audio track. Open a Bias cell, tap the red dot next to a lane, arm record on the transport, and press play. The song plays while you record, and the other lanes keep playing."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**When you stop, the take is cut at the scene lines.** One recording over the whole song becomes one cell per scene, all pointing at the same file, and cells are created in scenes where the track was empty. The full recording also stays in the sound library, so you can undo the split and place it by hand."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Trimming, fades, crossfades, flattening lanes, and tempo following are covered on **Bias**'s page. None of them change the file."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Takes can be up to half an hour. Anything over two minutes is converted once in the background and streamed from storage, so there may be a short wait the first time a long take is used."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Only one lane records at a time. Arming a second lane disarms the first."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "If the recorder fell behind and there's a gap in the take, it isn't split, and the whole take is kept in the library. If the transport never played, nothing was recorded against a scene, and the app tells you."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Machines that use audio"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Forage** - one sample per pad, with a filter, crusher and pitch envelope."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Mosaic** - zones across the keyboard and velocity, from your own files or a SoundFont."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Pollen** - grains from a file or the live input."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Dice** - a loop cut into slices."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Molt** - a sung take, tuned by the piano roll."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Cipher** and **Filament** can use the live input."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Bias** - four lanes of recordings along the song. Its recordings belong to its cells rather than to the machine."),
        )),
        ManualSection("Importing and exporting", "Getting songs and sounds in, and the song out.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Export…** in the file menu renders the song faster than real time, through the same engine that plays it, tails included."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "You can export the whole song, the current scene, or stems."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Formats"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**WAV** and **AIFF** - 16 or 24 bit, or 32-bit float."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**FLAC** - lossless and smaller."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**MP3** and **AAC** - at the bitrate you choose."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**MIDI** - the notes, not the sound. Drum tracks go on channel 10 as General MIDI drums, so other programs hear the right sounds."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**Song bundle** - the song and the samples it uses, in one file you can share."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Stems"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Stems writes each track to its own file in one pass, along with the full mix. Each mixer group is a stem too, and a track routed into a group is part of the group's stem instead of having its own, so the stems add up to the mix."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Loudness"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**loudness** is either **as mixed** or **-14 LUFS**. With -14, the song is rendered twice: once to measure it, then again with the gain that brings it to -14 LUFS, which is about where streaming services play things. The gain is held back if it would push the true peak over -1 dBTP, so a very dynamic song may end up a little under -14. Stems get the same gain, so they still add up to the mix."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The master strip in the mixer shows the same measurement while you play: integrated LUFS, and under it the short-term reading (**S**) and the true peak (**TP**). TP turns red above -1 dBTP. It resets every time you press play, or when you tap it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Exports are repeatable"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Exporting the same song twice gives identical files, because every machine is reset before the render starts."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Importing"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Import…** in the file menu takes a file from anywhere on the phone. What happens depends on what it is:"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**A MIDI file** opens a window that shows each part in the file with a machine chosen for it. Tap a machine to change it, or pick **skip** to leave the part out. Drums on channel 10 go to Hexbeat, with each drum moved to the matching sound. Parts that say what instrument they are get a fitting machine: an organ goes to Manual, brass to Brazen."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "The file is cut into scenes of 4, 8 or 16 bars. A stretch that's the same as the one before becomes a repeat, so a loop comes in as one scene played several times. The tempo and time signature come from the file."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "It opens as a new song and is saved straight away."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**A song bundle** (a .zip made by Export) opens as a song, with its samples. If you already have a sample with the same name, yours is kept and the bundle's comes in under a new name."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**A sound** (WAV, AIFF, FLAC or MP3) goes into the sound library, up to ten minutes of it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "An imported song never replaces one you've saved: if the name is taken, it gets a number after it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "Sharing"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "When an export finishes, **Share** sends it straight on through the phone's share sheet: email, Drive, a chat, or another app. Stems go as all their files together."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "**Share song…** in the file menu sends the open song as a bundle, samples included, for someone else to open in Acidulous."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The other way works too. Open a MIDI file, a bundle or a sound with Acidulous, or share one to it, and it goes wherever **Import…** would have put it."),
        )),
        ManualSection("Settings", "Settings for you and this phone, not the song.", listOf(
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "Nothing here is saved in a song, so opening someone else's song never changes these."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "display"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**theme** - dark, light, or follow the phone."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**interface size** - makes everything bigger, in four steps."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**screen while playing** - whether the screen can turn off while playing."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "audio"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**audio buffer** - tight, balanced or safe. Tight has the lowest latency but may crackle on a slower phone; safe gives the phone more time. The line under it shows the buffer size, the latency, and how many dropouts there have been."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**worst block** - the longest any block of audio took to make, against the time it had, and where that time went. The load meter is an average and can miss short spikes, which is why a phone can show a low load and still click. **reset** clears it. Blocks where the system briefly paused the audio thread aren't counted, and **% interrupted** shows how often that happened. If it's low and the worst block is high, the song is asking too much: freeze tracks or use **lean**. If it's high, the phone is busy with other things; closing other apps will help more."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**worst track** - what each track costs, most expensive first, so you know what to freeze. A **❄** means the track was frozen when it cost that much, which should be close to nothing. This is each track's worst block in a hundred, so one unlucky moment doesn't set it. Give it a few seconds of playing before trusting it."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**machine voice limit** - how many notes a track can hold at once. The oldest note is dropped first."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**quality** - what to give up when the phone can't keep up. **lean**: - runs the **amp** and **distortion** without oversampling (about half the amp's cost); - halves the **reverb**'s size (about half its cost); - halves the partials in **Resonance** patches that use more than twelve; - halves **Trinity**'s unison stacks (never below two), and lets at most **six released notes** ring at once, fading older tails out quickly. Held notes aren't affected; - halves the number of grains in **Pollen**. **auto** switches to lean by itself when the phone is struggling. Notes you're holding keep the quality they started with; only tails you've already let go of can be cut short. **Exports and freezing always use full quality.**"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Bullet, "**scheduler hint** - whether the phone accepted the app's request to treat the audio as time-critical. Some phones refuse; nothing to do about it here."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "record"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "The bit depth for recordings and exports."),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Heading, "songs"),
            ManualBlock(ManualKind.Para, "What a new song starts with: tempo, time signature, the first track's machine, and whether it starts with a scale set."),
        )),
    )
}
